---
import { REPOSITORY_URL, absoluteUrl } from "../../pages/_seo";

// The questions a developer actually asks in the first five minutes, in the
// order they ask them. Nothing here exists for a search engine: every entry is
// a question that has a precise answer, and the two that make Kivgraph look
// worse -- what grep is still better at, and what the viewer exposes -- are
// answered rather than omitted.
//
// The answers are plain strings because they are also the `acceptedAnswer` text
// of the FAQPage below, and structured data cannot carry markup.
const questions = [
  {
    question: "Does Kivgraph replace grep?",
    answer:
      "No, and the benchmark says where it should not. Over the 29 published questions grep cost fewer tokens on five of them, between 0.26x and 0.53x of ours, and both arms were fully correct on all five: three that ask whether a symbol has any caller, one two-hop impact question and one lookup of a name that occurs twice. Kivgraph is for type-checked references, transitive impact, consumers in another repository, and empty answers you can rely on.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Kivgraph save tokens?",
    answer:
      "Per question, yes. Over a whole agent session, it has not been demonstrated. On the current run, 29 questions cost 35,961 tokens over 36 tool calls through the graph against 267,980 tokens over 101 calls for grep plus reading, at precision 1.00 on both sides. Those are answers to isolated questions. Two end-to-end benchmarks, where an agent completes a task with and without the graph, disagree with each other and neither is conclusive: one measured 15% more cost with the graph (six tasks, kivgraph 0.3.0), the other 18% less (twelve fixes in this project's own Go repository, p = 0.146). The graph payloads are added to the context, and the agent does not always read less because of them.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does my code leave my machine?",
    answer:
      "No. The server is a local process, reached over stdio or over a loopback HTTP endpoint that requires a token, and no query path makes a network call off the machine. One thing to know: kivgraph ui, the optional viewer, opens a local HTTP listener that binds 127.0.0.1:7777 with no authentication. If you bind it to another address, it exposes repository paths, file paths, symbol names and signatures to whoever can reach it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does Kivgraph need an LLM, an API key or an embedding step?",
    answer:
      "None of the three. The edges are resolved by go/types, the TypeScript checker, rust-analyzer, the Dart analysis server and the Java and C# compiler indexers, all running locally. There is no model in the indexing path and no key to configure.",
  },
  {
    question: "How is this different from a vector database over my code?",
    answer:
      "A vector index answers what looks similar; this answers what is connected, and each edge records the analyzer that resolved it. The difference shows up in an empty answer: a similarity search always returns its nearest neighbours, whereas an empty reference list here is a claim that nothing calls the symbol.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does cross-repository analysis work?",
    answer:
      "Every registered repository is indexed into one graph, and each keeps its identity inside the keys it publishes, so a symbol used from outside the repository that declares it is a cross-repository consumer with its own evidence. In the published benchmark this found three of three consumers of one symbol where grep found two: the third re-exports it and never spells its name, so no text search reaches it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which languages are supported, and are they equal?",
    answer:
      "Go, TypeScript, Rust, Python, Dart, Java and C#, and no, they are not equal. Go, TypeScript and Rust edges are type-checked. Dart edges are resolved by the Dart Analysis Server. Java and C# edges come from their compilers' own indexers, scip-java and scip-dotnet. Python publishes exact semantic facts only when a configured Pyright-compatible analyzer provides them; with the bundled AST fallback its inferred references stay CANDIDATE and never become EXACT.",
  },
  {
    question: "What happens when Kivgraph finds no references?",
    answer:
      "It says so, and that is the answer rather than a failure. A reference that could not be resolved is published as UNRESOLVED with its reason, repository and language instead of being dropped, and CANDIDATE is reported as a different result from EXACT — so an empty list means nobody calls the symbol, not that nothing was found.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much does indexing cost, and how often must I re-index?",
    answer:
      "A rebuild is always a full pass that publishes a new generation; none is patched from the one before. What a second pass saves is analysis, through the fact cache. Measured cold with kivgraph 0.3.2 on the benchmark corpus of 37 repositories and 96,482 published symbols, one pass took 37.6 seconds and left 1,423 MB of on-disk state — the slowest and heaviest of the five tools compared. With a warm cache, a pass after a one-file edit took 17.2 seconds on a corpus of 53 repositories and 3.2 seconds on one repository of 12,465 symbols. A running server rebuilds when a registered repository changes; graph_status reports whether one has.",
  },
  {
    question: "What can it answer before I have indexed anything?",
    answer:
      "Nothing, deliberately. With no published generation the server registers only its three indexing controls and reports that it has no graph to answer from, rather than offering graph tools whose answers would be empty for the wrong reason.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  // Joined to the site graph by `@id` rather than left as an orphan script:
  // the two nodes the landing shell publishes are `#software` and `#website`.
  "@id": absoluteUrl(Astro.site, "/#faq"),
  isPartOf: { "@id": absoluteUrl(Astro.site, "/#website") },
  about: { "@id": absoluteUrl(Astro.site, "/#software") },
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<div class="space-y-3">
  {
    questions.map((item) => (
      <details class="border border-rule bg-panel px-5 py-4">
        <summary
          class="cursor-pointer font-mono text-sm font-medium text-gray-100"
        >
          {item.question}
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-sm leading-6 text-gray-300">
          {item.answer}
        </p>
      </details>
    ))
  }
</div>

<script
  is:inline
  type="application/ld+json"
  set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}
/>
<p class="mt-5 text-xs text-gray-400">
  More answers in the <a
    href="/kivgraph-faq/"
    class="text-accent-200 underline underline-offset-4">Kivgraph FAQ</a
  >, the <a
    href="/limits/"
    class="text-accent-200 underline underline-offset-4">limits</a
  > and on <a
    href={REPOSITORY_URL}
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
    class="text-accent-200 underline underline-offset-4">GitHub</a
  >.
</p>
